 much?\par 3.\emdash  [This] (}{\i\f211 today}{\f208 ) evening at uncle Gregory's we will be holding a surprise party (}{\i\f211 dance we-will-set-up}{\f208 ).\par 4. Our cousins Claudia and Cecilia will be there. [We will have great fun] (}{\i\f211 much we-will-be-amused}{\f208 ).\par 5.\emdash  I have no interest in dancing. I hate Claudia and Cecilia. They are real pests: they always [backbite about] (}{\i\f211 nibble}{\f208 ) everyone and everything.\par 6.\emdash  You won't be forced to dance, nor to speak with them.\par 7. [There will be a great buffet with the most varied foods and drinks] (}{\i\f211 Exquisite tables will be served up and on them foods and drinks in the greatest variety}{\f208 ): [caviar] (}{\i\f211 sturgeon's eggs}{\f208 ), hams, and every kind of cakes (cf. L 19, P 4).\par 8.\emdash  You (}{\i\f211 will}{\f208 ) tempt me in vain. I'm neither very given to drink nor gluttonous.\par 9.\emdash  [Do you enjoy music] (}{\i\f211 by-music are-you-pleased}{\f208 )?\par 10.\emdash  I enjoy music, but [I have no time to pay attention to it] (}{\i\f211 leisure to-me is-lacking to-it attention of-giving}{\f208 ).\par 11.\emdash  Unless [you come] (}{\i\f211 you-will-have-come}{\f208 ), the symphony will be out of tune. Marcel [plays] the trumpet (}{\i\f211 with-trumpet sings}{\f208 ); myself, I can beat the drum;\par 12. but [except for you I can find] nobody (}{\i\f211 except you I-find}{\f208 ) who can play the piano.\par 13.\emdash  Why didn't you say this first? I'll play the piano [for your sake] (}{\i\f211 by-grace of-you}{\f208 [}{\i\f211 pl}{\f208 .]).\par 14. It is most agreeable for me to be useful to [my] friends.\par \par NOTES\par }{\b\f210 1. Op}{\f208 o}{\b\f210 rtet}{\f208 , an impersonal verb that we've already seen in the infinitive (}{\b\f210 oport}{\f208 e}{\b\f210 re}{\f208 ) in Lesson 13 (P. 9). It is construed with the conjunction }{\b\f210 ut}{\f208  and the subjunctive: }{\b\f210 op}{\f208 o}{\b\f210 rtet ut v}