oes not enjoy music.\par 5. \emdash  Beware [of] the dog! \emdash  Where is the dog?\par 6. \emdash  I don't see the dog's master.\par 7. \emdash  Now he is bitten by a dog. Yesterday he was bitten by a dog.\par 8. \emdash  A dog bit him.\par 9. \emdash  [Don't give] ham to the dog (}{\i\f211 to-give do-not-want}{\f208 ).\par 10. \emdash  Dogs who bark the most, rarely bite.\par 11. \emdash  The monkey stole the dogs' food.\par 12. \emdash  They (f.) were bitten by many dogs.\par \par }{\b\f210 Attention}{\f208 ! }{\i\f211 Using the translation, check that you understand well phrases }{\b\i\f212 6}{\i\f211  and }{\b\i\f212 11}{\i\f211  of the exercise. If you immediately put the various nouns in their proper place (direct object, possessive phrase) it means you already have the Latin spirit. If you are not there yet, do not forget often to repeat this sort of exercise: what seems to you still to be artificial will become completely natural little by little. Think of: }{\b\i\f212 consuet}{\i\f211 u}{\b\i\f212 dinis m}{\i\f211 a}{\b\i\f212 gna vis}{\f208  (L 29, P 11).}{\par }}